 The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
 
-## Submission and Retrieval
+## Submission Envelope
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+A submission carries:
+- **Visit ID**: the UUID identifying the specific survey visit
+- **Base revision ID**: the server-assigned ID of the visit record the submitter started from (identifies the baseline for the edit)
+- **Visit record**: the complete edited state (all fields, author-assigned at submission time)
+- **Author ID**: the worker's authenticated identity at submission time
+- **Idempotency key**: client-generated UUID to prevent duplicates on retry
+- **Timestamp**: server receipt time
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+The server validates field types and plot references, then stores the complete original submission alongside its metadata. Identical resubmissions (same key and payload) return the original acceptance response without creating a duplicate stored submission.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Revision States and Current Visit
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+When a visit receives multiple competing edits, each becomes a distinct **revision**. Each revision is assigned a server-generated revision ID at acceptance time. The record store tracks:
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+- **Base revision**: the revision that serves as the edit baseline
+- **Competing revisions**: any later revisions sharing the same base
+- **Current revision**: the latest uncontested revision — no competing edits exist against its base
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+**Unresolved work**: a visit has unresolved review work when competing revisions exist (more than one revision with the same base revision). A visit with a single revision or a resolved chain of non-competing revisions has no review work.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+When retrieved, a visit's current state reflects the current revision. Any competing revisions are preserved and available for the review workflow.
+
+## Revision History and Chains
+
+A revision stores its base revision ID. This creates a chain: each submission builds on a specific prior state. The chain history is preserved to allow review decisions and to support audit trails. The current revision is the latest in any chain where no competing siblings exist.
+
+Example: Visit V1 submitted with base B1 (the initial submission) creates revision R1. A later edit from the same worker modifying R1 creates R2 (base = R1). A concurrent edit also from R1 creates R3 (base = R1). R2 and R3 compete; the system must hold both and await coordinator resolution. Once a coordinator selects R2 as current, R3 becomes a historical competitor and R2 becomes the base for future edits.
+
+## Resolution Operation
+
+A coordinator examines competing revisions and selects one to become the current revision. The operation records:
+- The competing revision IDs
+- The selected revision ID
+- The coordinator's identity
+- The resolution timestamp
+
+Future submissions must name the resolved revision (or its descendants) as their base to continue the chain uncontested. Submitting edits against an abandoned competing revision creates a new competing branch.
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Tablet-sync will use base revision IDs to detect conflicts — when a submission returns competing-revision feedback, the tablet shows the coordinator's competing versions for review. Review-export will exclude unresolved visits from the export set and provide a review queue showing competing revisions for coordinator resolution.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent edit handling | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | Each edit is preserved as a complete visit unit and reaches review as-submitted, avoiding rules that could silently combine related fields (e.g., species ID and cover estimate) without domain awareness. Review workload is higher, but correctness and auditability are prioritized over automation. |
+
+## Why Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Two credible approaches were evaluated:
+
+**Whole-visit revisions** (chosen) treats each submission as a complete snapshot. When two edits arrive against the same base, both are preserved and marked as competing. A coordinator reviews them side-by-side and selects one. This approach is simple to explain and audit: "here's what worker A submitted, here's what worker B submitted, which one should be current?" The trade-off is that *every* concurrent edit, even to unrelated fields, requires review. If one worker corrects an observer name while another adds a missing species, both edits are blocked until a coordinator decides which competing revision becomes current. The rationale is that in a 6,000-visit-per-season operation with two coordinators, auditability and correctness matter more than automation. Each submitted edit stays intact (no hidden combination). The chain of revisions and the coordinator's choice are traceable. Scientific accuracy is not compromised by merge rules that misunderstand domain relationships.
+
+**Field-aware three-way merge** (not chosen) would automatically combine edits to unrelated fields. The same scenario above — observer name vs. missing species — would merge automatically, neither edit would create review work, and both would flow to export. This reduces coordinator burden. The costs: (1) the record-store must maintain and enforce merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species tables — rules that do not understand whether a species ID change and a cover-estimate change are coordinated or independent; (2) automatically combined edits can silently cross scientific relationships the rules don't model (e.g., two edits to the same species row might be revising the ID vs. the coverage, and merging them could pair the wrong ID with the wrong coverage); (3) the tablet and export interfaces become more complex, needing to show field-level provenance and explain which edits were auto-combined vs. manually resolved.
+
+## Downstream Implications
+
+This choice shapes tablet-sync and review-export:
+
+**Tablet-sync must accommodate:**
+- Storing base revision IDs locally (not full snapshots) so edits can name the revision they're built on
+- Detecting when a submission returns competing-revision feedback and showing the coordinator what edits collided
+- Not treating a competing-revision response as an error, but as expected workflow
+- Allowing workers to continue offline even when review work is pending (the coordinator resolves it server-side; the tablet syncs the resolution on reconnect)
+
+**Review-export must accommodate:**
+- A review queue showing visits with unresolved competing revisions, paired with their full submission records
+- A side-by-side comparison view for coordinators to select the revision to keep
+- Excluding unresolved visits from the export set (no auto-combination fallback)
+- Recording and displaying which coordinator resolved which conflict and when
